","To calculate the dose of the drug, we first need to determine the patient's surface area in m². The formula for surface area is:

\[
\text{Surface area (m²)} = \sqrt{\frac{14 \times \text{weight (kg)} \times \text{height (m)}}{14 \times 14}}
\]

First, convert the patient's weight from pounds to kilograms:

\[
65 \text{ lb} \times \frac{1 \text{ kg}}{2.2 \text{ lb}} = 31.8 \text{ kg}
\]

Convert the height from feet and inches to meters:

\[
3 \text{ ft} \times 2.54 \text{ cm/ft} + 6 \text{ in.} \times 2.54 \text{ cm/in.} = 91.3 \text{ cm} = 0.913 \text{ m}
\]

Now, calculate the surface area:

\[
\text{Surface area (m²)} = \sqrt{\frac{14 \times 31.8 \times 0.913}{14 \times 14}} = 0.99 \text{ m²}
\]

Finally, multiply the surface area by the dose rate:

\[
\text{Dose (mg)} = 0.99 \text{ m²} \times 17.5 \text{ mg/m²/day} = 17.325 \text{ mg}
\]

Rounding to one decimal place, the dose should be \(\boxed{17.3 \text{ mg}}\).
