","Stimulation of arterial baroreceptors leads to an increase in the activity of neurons in the nucleus tractus solitatus (NTS). This is because the NTS is the primary site of origin for the sympathetic and parasympasympathetic responses to changes in blood pressure. The NTS receives sensory information from the baroreceptors via the baroreceptor reflex pathway, which includes the sympathetic and parasympasymphatic nervous systems. When blood pressure increases, the baroreceptors stretch and release norepinephrine, which stimulates the NTS neurons. This leads to a decrease in sympathetic activity and an increase in parasympasympathetic activity, which in turn causes a decrease in blood pressure.
