","To calculate the change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction, we can use the following equation:

ΔS = Σ(S_products) - Σ(S_reactants)

where S_products and S_reactants are the standard molar entropies of the products and reactants, respectively.

For the reaction:

3 CO(g) + 3/2 O2(g) → 3 CO2(g)

The standard molar entropies (S°) at 298 K are:

S°(CO) = 186.1 J/mol·K
S°(O2) = 205.1 J/mol·K
S°(CO2) = 213.6 J/mol·K

Now, we can plug these values into the equation:

ΔS = [3 × S°(CO2)] - [3 × S°(CO) + (3/2) × S°(O2)]

ΔS = [3 × 213.6 J/mol·K] - [3 × 186.1 J/mol·K + (3/2) × 205.1 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [640.8 J/mol·K] - [558.3 J/mol·K + 307.65 J/mol·K]

ΔS = [640.8 J/mol·K] - [865.95 J/mol·K]

ΔS = -224.15 J/mol·K

The change in entropy (ΔS) for the reaction is -224.15 J/mol·K.
