A **crystal structure** is a regular, repeating arrangement of atoms, ions, or molecules in a three-dimensional lattice. The symmetry of a crystal structure is determined by the way the atoms or molecules are arranged in the lattice and the symmetry operations that can be applied to the lattice without changing its overall structure.

The relationship between a space group and the symmetry of a crystal structure is that the **space group completely describes the symmetry of the crystal structure**. In other words, the symmetry operations of a space group are all the operations that can be applied to the crystal lattice to produce a structure that is identical to the original lattice. Conversely, any set of symmetry operations that can be applied to a crystal lattice to produce an identical structure can be represented by a single space group.

There are 23 distinct space groups, which are classified into 22 crystal systems and 1 additional class (the trithorite group). Each space group has a specific set of symmetry operations and a corresponding point group, which describes the symmetry of the lattice points. The **space group** is more comprehensive, as it includes the translations of the lattice points, which are not included in the point group.

