","The metal ion in the active site of hemoglobin is iron (Fe), and its coordination geometry is a distorted octahedral geometry. This geometry is crucial for the binding and release of oxygen molecules.

In the active site, the iron ion is coordinated to a porphyrin ring (heme group) and a nitrogen atom from a histidine residue (proximal histidine). The porphyrin ring has four nitrogen atoms that coordinate to the iron, while the proximal histidine provides a fifth coordination site. The sixth coordination site is available for binding to an oxygen molecule.

The distorted octahedral geometry allows for the binding of oxygen molecules in a reversible manner. When oxygen binds to the iron ion, it causes a slight change in the geometry of the heme group, which in turn leads to a conformational change in the protein. This change in conformation helps to stabilize the bound oxygen molecule and facilitates the binding of other oxygen molecules to other subunits of the hemoglobin protein.

When the oxygen concentration is low, the iron ion is in a higher oxidation state (Fe3+), and the oxygen binding site is less available. This makes it easier for the bound oxygen molecules to be released. Conversely, when the oxygen concentration is high, the iron ion is in a lower oxidation state (Fe2+), and the oxygen binding site is more available, facilitating the binding of oxygen molecules.

In summary, the distorted octahedral geometry of the iron ion in the active site of hemoglobin allows for reversible binding and release of oxygen molecules, which is essential for the proper functioning of the protein in transporting oxygen throughout the body.
