In a double-stranded DNA molecule, the bases pair up in a specific manner: adenine (A) pairs with thymine (T), and guanine (G) pairs with cytosine (C). This means that the number of adenines will equal the number of thymines, and the number of guanines will equal the number of cytosines.

Given the sequence of the one strand, the double-stranded DNA molecule will have the following composition:

Adenine (A): 20 (since A pairs with T)
Thymine (T): 20 (since T pairs with A)
Guanine (G): 25 (since G pairs with C)
Cytosine (C): 25 (since C pairs with G)

So, the total number of each base in the complete double-stranded DNA molecule is:

A = 20
G = 25
C = 25
T = 20

