","The terms ""isototopologues"" and ""isotopomers"" are used to describe molecules with different isotopic compositions. However, they refer to different types of isotopic substitutions:  
1. **Isototopologues**: These are molecules that have the same molecular formula but differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{H}_2\text{O} \), \( \text{D}_2\text{O} \), and \( ^3\text{H}_2\text{O} \) are isototopologues of water, as they all have the same molecular formula (\( \text{H}_2\text{O} \)) but differ in the isotopic composition of hydrogen (\( \text{H} \), \( \text{D} \), and \( ^3\text{H} \)).  
2. **Isotopomers**: These are molecules that have the same molecular formula and the same connectivity of atoms but differ in the isotopic composition of one or more atoms. For example, \( \text{H}_2\text{O} \) and \( \text{D}_2\text{O} \) are isotopomers of water, as they have the same connectivity (\( \text{H}_2\text{O} \)) but differ in the isotopic composition of hydrogen (\( \text{H} \) and \( \text{D} \)).  

Thus, the key difference between isototopologues and isotopomers is that isototopologues can have different connectivity of atoms, while isotopomers must have the same connectivity.  

\boxed{\text{Isototopologues: Same formula, different connectivity and isotopic composition.}}  
\boxed{\text{Isotopomers: Same formula, same connectivity, different isotopic composition.}}
