<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The Born-Lande equation is given by:
\[
U = \frac{2 \cdot e^2}{4 \pi \varepsilon_0 r} \cdot \left(1 - \frac{1}{n}\right)
\]
where:
- \( U \) is the lattice energy
- \( e \) is the electron charge (\( 1.602 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C} \))
- \( \varepsilon_0 \) is the vacuum permittivity (\( 8.854 \times 10^{-12} \, \text{C}^2/\text{N} \cdot \text{m}^2 \))
- \( r \) is the distance between the ions (0.231 nm = \( 2.31 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} \))
- \( n \) is the Madelung constant (for CaCl2, \( n = 1.747 \))
- \( 2.21 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{J nm} \) is the Born-Lande equation constant, which is \( 2.21 \times 10^{-19} \times 10^{-9} \, \text{J} = 2.21 \times 10^{-28} \, \text{J} \).

Substituting the values:
\[
U = \frac{2 \times (1.602 \times 10^{-19})^2}{4 \pi \times 8.854 \times 10^{-12}} \times \frac{1}{2.31 \times 10^{-10}} \times (1 - \frac{1}{1.747})
\]
\[
U = 2.21 \times 10^{-28} \times 1.747
\]
\[
U = 3.74 \times 10^{-28} \, \text{J}
\]
\[
U = 3.74 \times 10^{-28} \times 10^9 \, \text{J} \cdot \text{nm} = 3.74 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{nm}
\]
\[
U = 3.74 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{nm}
\]

Thus, the lattice energy of CaCl2 is \(\boxed{3.74 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{J} \cdot \text{nm}}\).

