","The molecular formula C4H10O can exhibit two types of isomerism: structural isomerism and optical isomerism.

1. Structural isomerism: Structural isomers have the same molecular formula but different arrangements of atoms in their molecular structure. In C4H10O, structural isomerism can be further classified into three types:
   a. Chain isomerism: This occurs when the carbon chain is arranged differently. For example, butanol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and 2-methylpropanol (CH3CH(OH)CH3) are chain isomers.
   b. Position isomerism: This occurs when the functional group (in this case, the hydroxyl group) is located at different positions on the carbon chain. For example, butanol (CH3CH2CH2CH2OH) and 2-butanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) are position isomers.
   c. Functional isomerism: This occurs when the molecule has different functional groups. For example, C4H10O can also form an ether, diethyl ether (CH3CH2OCH2CH3), which is a functional isomer of the alcohols mentioned above.

2. Optical isomerism: Optical isomers (enantiomers) are non-superimposable mirror images of each other. They occur when a molecule has a chiral center, which is a carbon atom bonded to four different groups. In C4H10O, only 2-butanol (CH3CH(OH)CH2CH3) has a chiral center at the second carbon atom, and thus exhibits optical isomerism.

In summary, the possible types of isomerism in C4H10O are structural isomerism (chain, position, and functional isomers) and optical isomerism (only for 2-butanol).
