","To prove \( B \subseteq A \), we set to show that every element \( b \in B \) is also in \( A \). 

1. Let \( b \in B \). 
2. Since \( A \triangle B \subseteq A \), and \( b \in B \), we have \( b \in A \triangle B \).
3. By the definition of symmetric difference, \( b \in A \triangle B \) implies \( b \in (A \setminus B) \cup (B \setminus A) \).
4. Since \( b \in B \), \( b \notin A \setminus B \). Thus, \( b \in B \setminus A \).
5. However, \( B \setminus A \) is the set of elements in \( B \) but not in \( A \), so \( b \in A \).

Thus, we have shown that any element \( b \in B \) is also in \( A \), so \( B \subseteq A \). 

**Final Answer:** \(\boxed{B \subseteq A}\)
