","(i) **Temperature after 5 minutes**:  
The lumped parameter method is used for heat transfer problems where the temperature is uniform throughout the body. The temperature of the part after 5 minutes can be determined using the following equation:  
\[
T(t) = T_0 + (T_a - T_0) \exp\left(-\frac{hA}{mc}t\right)
\]  
where:  
- \( T(t) \) = temperature at time \( t \)  
- \( T_0 \) = initial temperature = 345°C  
- \( T_a \) = ambient temperature = 20°C  
- \( h \) = convective heat transfer coefficient = 56.8 W/m²K  
- \( A \) = surface area = 0.075 m²  
- \( m \) = mass = 1.2 kg  
- \( c \) = specific heat = 385 J/kg K  
- \( t \) = time = 5 minutes = 300 s  

Substituting the values:  
\[
T(300) = 345 + (20 - 345) \exp\left(-\frac{56.8 \times 0.075}{1.2 \times 385} \times 300\right)
\]  
\[
T(300) = 345 + (-325) \exp(-0.54)
\]  
\[
T(300) = 345 - 325 \times 0.305
\]  
\[
T(300) = 345 - 99.75 = 245.25°C
\]  

