<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
The Arrhenius equation is given by:
\[
k = A e^{-E_a / RT}
\]
where \( k \) is the rate constant, \( A \) is the pre-exponential factor, \( E_a \) is the activation energy, \( R \) is the gas constant, and \( T \) is the temperature.

Taking the natural logarithm of both sides:
\[
\ln k = \ln A - \frac{E_a}{RT}
\]
The equation can be rearranged to solve for \( E_a \):
\[
E_a = -\frac{R \ln \left( \frac{k_2}{k_1} \right)}{1/T_2 - 1/T_1}
\]
Substituting the given values:
\[
E_a = -\frac{2 \ln \left( \frac{10^4}{10^{-1}} \right)}{0.0012 - 0.0018}
\]
\[
E_a = -\frac{2 \ln (10^5)}{-0.0006}
\]
\[
E_a = \frac{2 \times 5 \ln 10}{0.0006}
\]
\[
E_a = \frac{10 \ln 10}{0.0006}
\]
\[
E_a = \frac{10 \times 2.303}{0.0006}
\]
\[
E_a = \frac{23.03}{0.0006}
\]
\[
E_a = 38.38 \times 10^3 \, \text{cal K}^{-1} \, \text{mol}^{-1}
\]
\[
E_a = 38.38 \, \text{kcal K}^{-1} \, \text{mol}^{-1}
\]
Thus, the activation energy for the reaction is \(\boxed{38.38 \, \text{kcal K}^{-1} \, \text{mol}^{-1}}\).

