A concave spherical mirror has a radius of curvature of 25 cm. A point source of light is placed at a distance of 10 cm from the mirror's vertex along its principal axis. If the mirror has a diameter of 20 cm and a center thickness of 2 cm, what is the spherical aberration of the mirror?<end_of_turn>
<start_of_turn>model
","To find the spherical aberration of the mirror, we use the formula:

\[
\text{Aberration} = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{D^2}{4R} - \frac{D^2}{4R^2} \right)
\]

where:
- \( D \) is the diameter of the mirror (20 cm),
- \( R \) is the radius of curvature (25 cm).

Substituting the values:

\[
\text{Aberration} = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{20^2}{4 \times 25} - \frac{20^2}{4 \times 25^2} \right)
\]

\[
\text{Aberration} = \frac{1}{2} \left( \frac{400}{100} - \frac{400}{625} \right)
\]

\[
\text{Aberration} = \frac{1}{2} \left( 4 - 0.64 \right)
\]

\[
\text{Aberration} = \frac{1}{2} \times 3.36
\]

\[
\text{Aberration} = 1.68 \text{ cm}
\]

Thus, the spherical aberration of the mirror is \(\boxed{1.68 \text{ cm}}\).
