- \( \Delta \lambda \) is the change in wavelength,
- \( \hbar \) is the reduced Planck constant (\( \hbar = \frac{h}{2\pi} \approx 1.05457 \times 10^{-34} \, \text{J s} \)),
- \( m_e \) is the electron mass (\( m_e = 9.10938 \times 10^{-31} \, \text{kg} \)),
- \( c \) is the speed of light (\( c = 2.998 \times 10^{10} \, \text{cm/s} = 2.998 \times 10^8 \, \text{m/s} \)),
- \( \theta \) is the scattering angle.

The maximum change occurs when \( \theta = 180^\circ \), so \( \cos \theta = -1 \). Substituting \( \theta = 180^\circ \):

\[
\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}} = \frac{2 \pi \hbar}{m_e c} (1 + 1) = \frac{4 \pi \hbar}{m_e c}
\]

The percentage change is:

\[
\text{Percentage change} = \left( \frac{\Delta \lambda_{\text{max}}}{\lambda_0} \right) \times 100
\]

where \( \lambda_0 = 2.00 \, \text{Å} = 2.00 \times 10^{-10} \, \text{m} \).

