","(a) The proton is accelerated by the electric field, so the net force on the proton is \(F = qE\), where \(q\) is the charge of the proton and \(E\) is the electric field strength. Using Newton's second law, \(F = ma\), we can find the acceleration of the proton:  
\[ a = \frac{F}{m} = \frac{qE}{m} = \frac{(1.60 \times 10^{-19} \, \text{C})(500 \, \text{N/C})}{1.67 \times 10^{-27} \, \text{kg}} = 3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2. \]  
(b) The proton starts from rest, so its initial velocity is zero. The time \(t\) it takes to reach a speed \(v = 35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is given by:  
\[ v = at \implies t = \frac{v}{a} = \frac{35,000 \, \text{m/s}}{3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2} = 9.16 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}. \]  
(a) The magnitude of the acceleration is \(\boxed{3.81 \times 10^{11} \, \text{m/s}^2}\).  
(b) The time it takes the proton to reach a speed of \(35,000 \, \text{m/s}\) is \(\boxed{9.16 \times 10^{-8} \, \text{s}}\).
